{
  "summary": "The feature should make `pipeline tui` detect an existing tmux session (derived from `tmux_session_name()` in service.py) and attach to it via `tmux attach -t <session>` instead of launching a new Textual TUI instance. Three approaches were explored: (1) **Check-and-attach (recommended)**: Before launching the Textual app, check `tmux has-session -t <session>` — if it exists, exec into `tmux attach -t <session>` replacing the current process. This is the simplest and most predictable. (2) **Config-driven behavior**: Add a `tmux.auto_attach: true` config option so users can opt in/out, with the session name either auto-derived or explicitly set via `tmux.session_name` in config.json. (3) **Force-new flag**: Add `pipeline tui --new` to bypass the attach behavior and always start a fresh instance. Key considerations: the attach should use `os.execvp()` rather than `subprocess.run()` so the original process is fully replaced; the check should handle the case where tmux isn't installed (gracefully fall back to launching a new TUI); and the TUI lock mechanism should be respected — if attaching to an existing session, no new lock should be acquired. A config option like `tmux.session_name` or `tmux.auto_attach` would give users explicit control. The implementation is minimal — roughly 15 lines added to the `tui()` click command in pipeline.py before the `run_tui()` call."
}
